  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 329 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nBroad upper ridging remains in place over the southwestern CONUS with\na modest jet across the northern tier, leaving northeast Kansas \nunder relatively weak flow aloft and subsidence. A modest cold front \nthat moved through the area recently has ushered in a cooler and \ndrier boundary layer, with clear skies and light winds observed. \nThis pattern supports efficient radiational cooling at night and \nwell-mixed, seasonable afternoons.\n\nFor the near term, the main forecast issues are temperatures and\nlocalized fog. The combination of clear skies, light winds, and \nrelatively moist low levels in river valleys may support shallow \nground fog toward daybreak, especially near the Kansas River basin \nand other local drainages. Any fog that develops is expected to \nremain patchy and shallow, with limited spatial extent and rapid \ndissipation after sunrise. Modest warm advection begins on the \nbackside of the retreating surface ridge as low-level flow veers to a\nmore southerly component this afternoon. \n\nThe midweek period features continued quiet conditions as another \nweak frontal boundary brushes the area from the north while upper- \nlevel flow backs slightly. Ahead of this front, low-level warm \nadvection strengthens, and highs trend into the upper 70s to lower \n80s across much of the area, with lows climbing into the upper 50s to\nlower 60s.The frontal passage will likely be dry across northeast \nKansas, and large-scale ascent remains weak. As a result, \nprecipitation chances are negligible, and sky cover stays generally \nlow to moderate with a mix of sun and occasional thin cloud. \nNortherly to easterly winds behind the boundary are expected to \nremain modest, with speeds mostly under 15 mph. No meaningful impacts\nare anticipated from this frontal passage beyond modest day-to-day \ntemperature variations.\n\nThrough the end of the forecast period, the synoptic pattern \ncontinues to favor ridging or weak zonal flow over the central \nPlains. Warm advection and subsidence support above-normal \ntemperatures, with many locations likely seeing highs in the low 80s \nand mild nights in the lower to mid 60s. The airmass remains \nrelatively dry, and the lack of substantial forcing or deep moisture \nsuggests precipitation remains absent through at least Saturday.",
